3. Public corresponding source
If you distribute a modified version, or make a modified version available to
others through a network service, you must, from the time it is made available,
publicly provide its complete corresponding source at a stable URL, without
payment or a login requirement, under this same license. Clearly display that
URL with the distribution and, for a network service, in its interface.
Corresponding source means the preferred form for modification, including your
changes, required build and installation scripts, dependency declarations,
and sanitized example configuration needed to build and run that version.
Do not include passwords, tokens, private keys, account data, or private logs.
Document any omitted private configuration so users can supply their own.
Keep the source available while distributing or operating that version and
for at least three years after the last such distribution or operation.
Private modifications that are neither distributed nor used to provide a
service to others do not trigger this source-publication requirement.
For unmodified executable distributions, provide the corresponding source
with the distribution or a publicly accessible URL for that exact version.

